\chapter{Immutable and mutable bindings}
A binding gives a name to a value in an environment. Use \api{val} when the
name should retain its initial value, and \api{var} when later assignment is
part of the program. Both require an initializer.

\begin{mullcode}
val rate <- 3;
var total <- 0;
total <- total + rate;
total <- total + rate;
G:Assert(total == 6);
{ val rate <- 10; G:Assert(rate == 10); }
G:Assert(rate == 3);
\end{mullcode}

\section{Scope and assignment}
Braces introduce lexical scopes. A declaration may shadow an outer name but
cannot duplicate a name in the same scope. Assignment searches outward for
the nearest binding and requires that binding to be mutable. Assigning an
undefined name is an error; it does not create a global implicitly.

Assignment uses the same arrow as initialization, but has no declaration
keyword. It is a statement, not an expression that can be nested inside
another expression. The target must be a binding name. Record members and
list indexes are read-only; use library functions to construct updated values.

\section{Persistence}
Top-level bindings persist between calls to a runtime's \api{run} method.
Running the same top-level \api{val} declaration twice therefore reports a
duplicate binding. Use a braced scope for temporary calculations, a function
for reusable logic, or a new runtime for an independent session. A runtime
error preserves mutations already performed before the error; execution is
not a transaction.
